## 7. Quantitative suitability / excessive trading concerns

This pattern also raises quantitative suitability concerns. Although “churning” traditionally requires broker control, excessive trading and excessive switching can violate suitability and Reg BI even without classic discretionary control if the customer relied heavily on the broker’s recommendations.

Fourteen switches over 24 months in a conservative retirement account is a red flag. The transactions generated more than 11% of the account value in sales loads. That is likely excessive relative to the Kowalskis’ profile and objectives.

If Brian effectively controlled the account through his recommendations — which is common where elderly, unsophisticated retirees follow an advisor’s advice — the conduct could also support a churning or excessive-trading claim.

